---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const { lang = 'en' } = Astro.props;

const title = "About ConvertOcean | Who Builds It and How It's Tested";
const description = "Who builds ConvertOcean, how every converter is tested before it ships, what the tools cannot do yet, and how to check the no-upload claim yourself.";

const aboutSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  "name": "About ConvertOcean",
  "url": "https://convertocean.com/about/",
  "mainEntity": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "ConvertOcean",
    "url": "https://convertocean.com",
    "logo": "https://convertocean.com/web-app-manifest-512x512.png",
    "foundingDate": "2026",
    "description": "An independent, privacy-first suite of client-side file converters, PDF utilities, and business calculators that run entirely in the browser with no server uploads."
  }
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} category="About Us" categorySlug="about">
  <style>
    .about-container {
      max-width: 800px;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }
    .about-lead {
      font-size: 20px;
      color: var(--colors-body);
      line-height: 1.6;
      margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
    }
    .about-section {
      margin-bottom: var(--spacing-2xl);
    }
    .about-section-title {
      font-size: 22px;
      font-weight: var(--fw-bold);
      letter-spacing: -0.6px;
      margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
      color: var(--colors-ink);
    }
    .about-text {
      font-size: 15.5px;
      color: var(--colors-body);
      line-height: 1.7;
      margin-bottom: var(--spacing-md);
    }
    .values-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--spacing-lg);
      margin: var(--spacing-xl) 0;
    }
    @media (max-width: 600px) {
      .values-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
    }
    .value-card {
      background-color: var(--colors-canvas);
      border: 1px solid var(--colors-hairline);
      border-radius: var(--rounded-md);
      padding: var(--spacing-lg);
      transition: transform 0.2s, border-color 0.2s;
    }
    .value-card:hover {
      transform: translateY(-2px);
      border-color: var(--colors-hairline-strong);
    }
    .value-title {
      font-size: 16px;
      font-weight: var(--fw-bold);
      color: var(--colors-ink);
      margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xs);
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: var(--spacing-xxs);
    }
    .value-text {
      font-size: 13.5px;
      color: var(--colors-body);
      line-height: 1.6;
    }
  </style>

  <main id="main-content" tabindex="-1" class="app-container" style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: var(--spacing-xl) var(--spacing-lg) var(--spacing-4xl); position: relative; z-index: 1;">
    <div class="about-container">
      <div class="page-header">
        <h1 class="page-title">About ConvertOcean.</h1>
        <p class="about-lead">ConvertOcean is a set of file converters, PDF tools and business calculators that run inside your browser tab. The code that reads and converts your file runs on your own device, so the file is never sent to a server.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h2 class="about-section-title">Why it exists</h2>
        <p class="about-text">
          Most online converters work the same way: your file is copied to their server, converted there, and deleted later, often "after an hour". That is fine for a meme. It is a real risk for a payslip, a signed contract, a bank statement or a passport scan, and you have no way to check what actually happened to the copy.
        </p>
        <p class="about-text">
          Browsers can now do most of that work themselves: read a Word file, draw a PDF, decode an iPhone photo. ConvertOcean started in June 2026 to find out how far that goes. Where a browser can do the job well, the tool is here. Where it cannot, the page says so instead of quietly sending your file somewhere else.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h2 class="about-section-title">Who builds it</h2>
        <p class="about-text">
          ConvertOcean is built and maintained by an independent developer, not a company. There is no investor and no paid tier. The site is paid for by ads and uses anonymous analytics, and neither of them ever sees your files.
        </p>
        <p class="about-text">
          All of the code is public on <a href="https://github.com/Kavyansh-55/ConvertOcean" style="color: var(--colors-link);" rel="noopener">GitHub</a> under the MIT licence, so every claim on this page can be checked against the code that runs. Bug reports go to the <a href="/contact/" style="color: var(--colors-link);">contact page</a> or to <a href="https://github.com/Kavyansh-55/ConvertOcean/issues" style="color: var(--colors-link);" rel="noopener">GitHub Issues</a>, and the person who reads them is the person who fixes them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h2 class="about-section-title">Check the privacy claim yourself</h2>
        <p class="about-text">
          You do not have to take this on trust. On any tool page, open your browser's developer tools, go to the <strong>Network</strong> tab, and convert a file. No request carries your file: the only traffic is the page's own scripts, analytics and ads.
        </p>
        <p class="about-text">
          For a stronger test, load a tool page, switch your computer to airplane mode, and convert anyway. It still works, because the conversion never needed the internet.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h2 class="about-section-title">How the tools are tested</h2>
        <p class="about-text">
          Every converter is checked by opening the real tool page in a real browser and feeding it deliberately awkward files: Word documents with section breaks and tables, phone photos stored sideways with a rotation flag, spreadsheets with hidden columns, and PDFs in scripts other than English. The output is then read back and compared with what it should contain.
        </p>
        <div class="values-grid">
          <div class="value-card">
            <h3 class="value-title">300+ conversion checks</h3>
            <p class="value-text">
              All of them have to pass before a converter change goes live. After each release, a separate set of checks runs against the live site to confirm it serves what was tested.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="value-card">
            <h3 class="value-title">More than file output</h3>
            <p class="value-text">
              Separate test suites cover layouts at four phone and tablet widths, Apple's Safari engine, offline use, very large files, and what happens when a script library fails to load.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="value-card">
            <h3 class="value-title">Calculators shown in full</h3>
            <p class="value-text">
              The <a href="/profit-margin-calculator/" style="color: var(--colors-link);">profit margin</a>, <a href="/break-even-calculator/" style="color: var(--colors-link);">break-even</a> and <a href="/sales-tax-calculator/" style="color: var(--colors-link);">sales tax</a> calculators use the standard formulas and print them on the page, so any result can be checked by hand.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="value-card">
            <h3 class="value-title">Reports come first</h3>
            <p class="value-text">
              When someone reports a wrong result, fixing it takes priority over building anything new, and a test is added so the same mistake cannot come back unnoticed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h2 class="about-section-title">What the tools can't do yet</h2>
        <p class="about-text">
          Working only inside the browser has limits. These are the ones you are most likely to meet:
        </p>
        <ul class="about-text" style="padding-left: 1.2em;">
          <li><strong>Old Office formats.</strong> Legacy .doc and .ppt files (Office 97–2003) cannot be read in a browser. Open the file in Word, PowerPoint or LibreOffice, save it as .docx or .pptx, then convert.</li>
          <li><strong>Some writing systems in PDFs.</strong> Text, CSV and Excel to PDF cannot yet draw Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Arabic, Hebrew, Thai or some Indian scripts. When a file contains them, the tool tells you rather than producing blank boxes.</li>
          <li><strong>Excel print settings.</strong> <a href="/excel-to-pdf/" style="color: var(--colors-link);">Excel to PDF</a> prints the cells that hold data. It ignores a print area you set in Excel, and hidden columns still print.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="about-text">
          The calculators are informational tools, not professional advice. They are built to be mathematically correct, but check pricing, tax and filing decisions with an accountant or your local tax authority.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(aboutSchema)}></script>
</Layout>
